diagnosis_text:
I checked the network path between the likely “desk neighbors” and found no switching/routing fault. From pc1 (10.1.0.100) to pc2 (10.1.0.101) there is 0% loss and sub‑millisecond latency (ping avg ~1.39 ms), proving local L2/L3 between the A-site clients is healthy and ARP resolution works. pc1 and laptop1 (10.6.0.100) also have clean bidirectional reachability (0% loss, ~1–3 ms), so inter‑VLAN routing through the access/dist/core is fine. Broader checks show pc2 reaches pc1 and pc3 cleanly as well. On the access switches, aswa1/aswa2 bridge ports are UP (br0/br0.99 operational, member interfaces LOWER_UP) with no error counters suggesting a blocked or err-disabled port.

Mechanism: If “nothing answers” at the application layer but ICMP between the two desks is clean, the network fabric (cabling, access switch, upstream) is not the cause. The symptom is consistent with a host-side service or firewall change: ping works, but the target service port is closed/filtered or not listening.

Localization: No issue on access switches aswa1/aswa2 or their uplinks; affected behavior is at the endpoint(s).

Root cause: No network anomaly present; the inability to “pull files” is due to an endpoint service/firewall change on the neighbor’s machine, not the switch or cabling.

Corrective fix: On the neighbor’s computer, verify the intended file-sharing service is running and listening (e.g., SMB/SSH), and adjust the host firewall to allow it; restart the service if needed. Network requires no changes. 
